<Layout title="Designing Dashboards That CFOs Actually Read">
    <FullBlogPost
        title="Designing Dashboards That CFOs Actually Read"
        date="Coming soon"
        readTime="9 mins read"
        category="Reporting · Finance"
        author="James Nguyen"
    >
        <p class="lede">
            The dashboards I built for Day &amp; Night Solar leadership get opened every day.
            Here's how I stopped designing for dashboard-lovers and started designing for people
            who just need the number.
        </p>

        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
            Most dashboards fail the same way: too many charts, no hierarchy, and the story
            buried in filters. The CFO wants the answer on page one and the trail underneath.
        </p>

        <h2>What you'll build</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>A pandas + openpyxl pipeline that auto-regenerates</li>
            <li>A dashboard template covering P&amp;L, cash flow, billing, margin, and schedule</li>
            <li>A "static HTML operations hub" with collapsible sections and clickable file links</li>
            <li>An update cadence that doesn't require you to babysit it</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>1. The one-screen rule</h2>
        <p><em>Coming soon — what goes above the fold and what doesn't.</em></p>

        <h2>2. Generating the Excel report from pandas</h2>
        <p><em>Coming soon — openpyxl patterns that actually look like a CFO report.</em></p>

        <h2>3. Operations hubs as HTML</h2>
        <p><em>Coming soon — a single static file leadership can open instead of ten tabs.</em></p>

        <h2>Wrap-up</h2>
        <p><em>Coming soon — what's measured on adoption, and the tweaks that moved the number.</em></p>
    </FullBlogPost>
</Layout>

